Hi -- you can change the size of the PDF, or of the pie charts.  But that won't help with the "so many slices it's looking black" issue.

As you say, that genuinely reflects the uncertainty of the estimate for those nodes. That's a result, conditional on the model and data and tree, the only thing that will change it is changing the model or data (or tree).  E.g. it could be that you have over-split the areas given your number of species.  If a bunch of species are widespread, it's common to have uncertainty, as there are many ways to have multi-area ranges, and many ways to convert between them.  But if you collapse it to (e.g.) just North and South, or some such, you might get more clarity for that coarse question.

The other option is a per-area probabilities plot, this can be useful in these situations:

http://phylo.wikidot.com/example-biogeobears-scripts#per-area_probabilities
http://phylo.wikidot.com/example-biogeobears-scripts#per-area_probabilities_graphics

Cheers,
Nick

